    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "Selecting a micro-frontend pattern requires evaluating trade-offs across multiple dimensions: team autonomy, performance, operational complexity, developer experience, and organizational maturity. No pattern is universally superior-each optimizes for different constraints and values. The decision framework should weigh these dimensions against your specific context."
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "Team autonomy varies dramatically across patterns. Module Federation provides moderate autonomy-teams deploy independently but must coordinate framework versions and shared dependencies. Runtime integration offers high autonomy, enabling polyglot architectures at the cost of duplication and complexity. Build-time integration sacrifices autonomy for simplicity, requiring coordination on release timing. Web Components and Server-Side Composition fall in the middle, allowing technical independence (different frameworks) while requiring contract adherence (Web Component APIs, fragment response formats)."
